WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.400
A circuit is built in development.

2
00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:10.420
If a child is born without one of its proteins, is the circuit wrong for good?

3
00:00:11.433 --> 00:00:16.253
Autism is diagnosed from behaviour, not from a gene, blood or scan test.

4
00:00:17.267 --> 00:00:21.367
Yet about a hundred genes now have strong evidence for a role in autism.

5
00:00:22.417 --> 00:00:24.077
Many fall into three jobs.

6
00:00:24.917 --> 00:00:26.357
Some build and hold the synapse.

7
00:00:27.317 --> 00:00:29.497
Some set how much protein a synapse makes.

8
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.700
And some set which genes the neuron uses.

9
00:00:33.783 --> 00:00:36.083
The three jobs seem to meet at the synapse.

10
00:00:36.923 --> 00:00:40.163
Each can change what it is made of, and how strongly it responds.

11
00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:44.407
Much of building happens early, in windows that close.

12
00:00:45.467 --> 00:00:49.807
Picture a mouse born without one synapse protein, with symptoms by young adulthood.

13
00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:54.077
One history: the protein was needed to build the circuit.

14
00:00:54.077 --> 00:00:57.537
The circuit was built wrong, and the window has closed.

15
00:00:58.517 --> 00:01:01.317
Another history: the circuit was built well enough.

16
00:01:02.057 --> 00:01:03.777
But it cannot run without the protein.

17
00:01:04.817 --> 00:01:06.897
The symptoms look the same either way.

18
00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:11.687
Predict. You can switch the gene on or off at any age.

19
00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:14.967
Which two late experiments would tell these histories apart?

20
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.720
First, put the gene back after symptoms begin.

21
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:28.620
Second, take it away from a typical mouse, after its circuit is built.

22
00:01:29.583 --> 00:01:34.363
If the protein was needed only to build, and the damage cannot be repaired, both

23
00:01:34.363 --> 00:01:35.363
tests do nothing.

24
00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:40.047
If it keeps the circuit running, restoring it helps, and removing it harms.

25
00:01:41.117 --> 00:01:44.857
If it does both, restoring it may help fully, partly or not at all.

26
00:01:45.697 --> 00:01:46.777
Removing it still harms.

27
00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:53.130
A failed rescue is hard to read: the gene may have come back in too few cells, at

28
00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:56.330
the wrong level or too briefly, or the window may have closed.

29
00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:00.210
Deletion asks whether the adult circuit still needs the gene.

30
00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:02.110
You need both tests.

31
00:02:03.083 --> 00:02:05.063
Carry one idea through this lesson.

32
00:02:05.063 --> 00:02:10.663
Built, then kept. A synapse gene can be needed to build a circuit, to keep it

33
00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:11.683
running, or both.

34
00:02:12.583 --> 00:02:15.503
Takeaway: restore the gene late, and delete it late.

35
00:02:16.403 --> 00:02:18.103
One asks if a trait can recover.

36
00:02:18.983 --> 00:02:21.603
The other asks if the adult circuit still needs it.
